## Proof

**Definitions.** A *closure operator* on a set $S$ is a map $\operatorname{cl}\colon \mathcal{P}(S)\to\mathcal{P}(S)$ satisfying, for all $A,B\subseteq S$:

1. **(Extensive)** $A\subseteq \operatorname{cl}(A)$,
2. **(Monotone)** $A\subseteq B \;\Longrightarrow\; \operatorname{cl}(A)\subseteq \operatorname{cl}(B)$,
3. **(Idempotent)** $\operatorname{cl}(\operatorname{cl}(A))=\operatorname{cl}(A)$.

A closure operator is **algebraic** if for every $A\subseteq S$,
$$\operatorname{cl}(A)=\bigcup_{\substack{F\subseteq A\\ F\text{ finite}}}\operatorname{cl}(F).$$

A closure system is **finite** if the underlying set $S$ is finite. We prove:

> **Theorem.** Every closure operator on a finite set is algebraic.

---

**Proof.** Let $S$ be a finite set and let $\operatorname{cl}$ be a closure operator on $S$. Fix an arbitrary $A\subseteq S$. We establish the equality
$$\operatorname{cl}(A)=\bigcup_{\substack{F\subseteq A\\ F\text{ finite}}}\operatorname{cl}(F)$$
by proving both inclusions.

**($\supseteq$)** Let $F\subseteq A$ be any finite subset. By monotonicity, $\operatorname{cl}(F)\subseteq \operatorname{cl}(A)$. Since this holds for every finite $F\subseteq A$,
$$\bigcup_{\substack{F\subseteq A\\ F\text{ finite}}}\operatorname{cl}(F)\;\subseteq\;\operatorname{cl}(A).$$

**($\subseteq$)** Because $S$ is finite, every subset of $S$ is finite; in particular $A$ itself is finite. Taking $F=A$ (which is a finite subset of $A$), the family $\{F\subseteq A : F\text{ finite}\}$ contains $A$ as a member. Hence $\operatorname{cl}(A)$ is one of the terms appearing in the union:
$$\operatorname{cl}(A)\;\subseteq\;\bigcup_{\substack{F\subseteq A\\ F\text{ finite}}}\operatorname{cl}(F).$$

Combining both inclusions yields $\operatorname{cl}(A)=\bigcup_{F\subseteq A,\,F\text{ finite}}\operatorname{cl}(F)$. Since $A\subseteq S$ was arbitrary, $\operatorname{cl}$ is algebraic. $\blacksquare$

---

**Remark.** The argument uses only two ingredients: monotonicity (for $\supseteq$) and the fact that finiteness of $S$ forces every $A\subseteq S$ to be finite, so $A$ itself serves as the "witness" finite subset (for $\subseteq$). Thus the algebraicity condition, which for infinite sets is a genuine restriction requiring closure to be determined by finitely generated sub-closures, becomes vacuous on finite sets: the supremum over all finite sub-closures already includes the closure of $A$ itself.

